# Six flat shapes, named from what each one is like, what is it, and how many with a plural.
lesson language-shapes v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=6 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Circles, squares and stars"
  goal "Name six shapes in [[language.name]], ask what something is, and count shapes in [[language.name]]."

  look {
    scene 25x5 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row all-shapes space=1 align=end {
          props s1 count=1 prop=circle
          props s2 count=1 prop=square
          props s3 count=1 prop=triangle
          props s4 count=1 prop=rectangle
          props s5 count=1 prop=pentagon
          props s6 count=1 prop=hexagon
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      Each shape has a name: [[shape.the-circle]], [[shape.the-square]], [[shape.the-triangle]],
      [[shape.the-rectangle]], [[shape.the-pentagon]] and [[shape.the-hexagon]]. More than one
      circle is [[shape.three-circles]], three circles. To ask what something is, say
      [[shape.what-is-it]], what is it? [[shape.the-star|cap]] is a star and [[shape.the-heart]] a
      heart.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.shapes-name k=3
    }
    show language.shapes-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.shapes-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.shapes-write k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    show language.shapes-name k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.shapes-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.shapes-write k=4 hard-k=2
    show language.shapes-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.shapes-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.shapes-write k=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.shapes-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.shapes-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[shape.the-circle|cap]], [[shape.the-square]], [[shape.the-triangle]], [[shape.the-rectangle]], [[shape.the-pentagon]], [[shape.the-hexagon]]. [[shape.what-is-it]] What is it?"
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is the square and the rectangle taken for each other, and the
    pentagon and the hexagon: every naming question offers the shape most often mixed up, and the
    feedback asks the child to count the sides. The tiles are drawn from the shelf's shapes, the
    same way each time, so the picture carries the shape and the child's job is the word. The step
    up from the numbers of grades one and two is a number counting a plural noun, three circles, and
    a question that asks what a thing is. The listening question is a dictation: read the sentence
    aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or let the device play it. It is marked
    word by word, and a missing accent counts there, while the words typed on their own in this
    lesson are taken without their accents. If the child is stuck, cut the shapes from paper and
    sort them, naming each. Off the page, go on a shape hunt round the house, naming what you find.
    It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7):
    "broaden their vocabulary and develop their ability to understand new words that are introduced
    into familiar written material"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language activities: "Activities
    to talk about the numbers and figures, etc. of surroundings while showing the actual objects"
    (Japan's grade three book Let's Try 1 has a unit on shapes, recalled); and CEFR A1, "Can
    understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[shape.the-rectangle]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[shape.the-circle]] and [[shape.the-square]] and the words to write are [[shape.triangle]] and [[shape.pentagon]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[shape.the-pentagon]] and [[shape.the-hexagon]] and the words to write are [[shape.rectangle]], [[shape.triangle]] and [[shape.square]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
